    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Longshan culture</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yellow-river.jpg" alt="Yellow River" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Yellow River <span class="kid-tale-cr">fading, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>About 5,000 years ago, farmers lived along the Yellow River in northern China.</p><p>Their culture is named after Longshan, which means “Dragon Mountain”, a town near where its remains were found in 1928.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/millet.jpg" alt="Millet" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Millet <span class="kid-tale-cr">Kurt Stüber [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They grew millet above all, with some rice and wheat.</p><p>Pigs gave them most of their meat, and they also kept sheep, goats and dogs.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silk.jpg" alt="Silk" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Silk <span class="kid-tale-cr">Kuebi = Armin Kübelbeck, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They raised silkworms and made a little silk.</p><p>Potters used a fast-turning wheel to make shiny black pots so thin they are called egg-shell pottery.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>As the number of people grew, about thirty towns were ringed with walls of rammed earth, earth pressed down hard.</p><p>A weapon called the dagger-axe shows that fighting became more common.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>People heated the shoulder bones of animals to try to learn hidden things, a practice called divination.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/copper.jpg" alt="Copper" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Copper <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jonathan Zander; derivative work by Materialscientist, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Late on, metalworkers made copper tools, rings and bells, leading the way to the Bronze Age.</p><p>About 4,000 years ago many big towns were left empty, but in central Henan the culture grew into the Erlitou culture.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Longshan culture is named after the town of Longshan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, near the Chengziya site found in 1928, and is best known for thin, polished black pottery made on the potter’s wheel.</li><li>In the 3rd millennium BCE its population grew sharply: towns were walled with rammed earth, a few centres grew to over 200 hectares, and the dagger-axe points to more frequent warfare.</li><li>Around 2000 BCE its population fell sharply and large settlements were abandoned, except in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, where the culture developed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erlitou-culture">Erlitou culture</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Some Longshan men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> stood between 180 and 190 cm tall, taller than any other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> population in the world; the tallest yet found, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>, was a youth of about 16–18 who measured 193 cm.</p></aside>
    </section>

